.TH RUNFLOW 1 "2026-10-16" "runflow" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
runflow \- the execution environment for OPM: run Flow and its tools, locally or through a scheduler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY runflow
.RI [ OPTION ]...\& " SCRIPT " [ ARG ]...
.SY runflow
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B \-c
.IR COMMAND " [" ARG ]...
.SY runflow
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.B \-\-shell
.SY runflow
.RI [ OPTION ]...\&
.BR \-\-jobs [ =all ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runflow
is the execution environment for OPM (Open Porous Media).
It runs Flow, Flow's Python and other programs the same way on a workstation and on a cluster: it finds the installed Flow versions and the Python each one needs, and starts MPI runs with the launcher of the scheduler in use.
It is work in progress: until version 1.0, a new version may change or remove functionality and syntax (CHANGELOG.md lists each change).
.PP
It runs
.I SCRIPT
with bash, with its
.IR ARG s
as
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
\&..., or, with
.BR \-c ,
a single
.IR COMMAND .
Both use
.IR directives ,
bash functions whose names start with
.BR @ :
.TP 12
.B @flow
run Flow (one process)
.TP
.B @flowpy
run the Python of a Flow version, with its opm bindings
.TP
.B @mpi
run a program, or
.BR @flow ,
with MPI
.TP
.B @submit
queue a command or directive as a job
.TP
.BR @wait ", " @cancel
wait for or cancel jobs
.TP
.B @help
list the directives, or show the options of one
.PP
Directives compose:
.B "@submit \-n 16 @mpi @flow CASE.DATA"
queues a job that runs Flow on 16 MPI ranks.
Only
.B @submit
uses a scheduler: the built-in local queue
.BR localq ;
LSF, SLURM and PBS are planned
.RB ( \-\-dry\-run
shows what a workflow would submit to them).
.PP
Options given to
.B runflow
are defaults for every directive in the script; options on a directive override or add to them.
For example, with this
.IR run.sh :
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
@mpi @flow "$1" && @flowpy summary.py FOPR "$1"
.EE
.PP
.B "runflow \-n 8 \-\-flow\-version 2025.04 run.sh CASE.DATA"
runs
.I CASE.DATA
with Flow 2025.04 on 8 MPI ranks, then reads its oil production.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command
Run the remaining arguments as a command.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-np " \fIN\fR"
Default number of processes (default 1).
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " \fIQ\fR"
Default queue (default: see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-scheduler " \fIS\fR"
.B auto
(default) or
.BR localq ;
.BR lsf ,
.B slurm
and
.B pbs
are planned:
.B \-\-dry\-run
only.
.TP
.BR \-\-depends\-on " \fIID\fR[,\fIID\fR]..."
Make every submitted job wait for these jobs.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-localq\-options ", " \-\-lsf\-options ", " \-\-slurm\-options ", " \-\-pbs\-options " \fIOPTS\fR"
Native options for every job sent to that scheduler (see
.BR "OPTION LAYERS" ).
.TP
.BI \-\- DIRECTIVE \-options " OPTS"
Options for every invocation of a directive, e.g.
.BR \-\-flow\-options ,
.BR \-\-mpi\-options .
.TP
.BI \-\- SETTING " VALUE"
A setting of a directive, e.g.
.BI \-\-flow\-version " VERSION"\fR:\fP
the Flow version for every
.B @flow
without
.BR \-v .
.BI "@help @" NAME
shows a directive's settings.
.TP
.BI \-\-directives " PATH"
Load extension directives from a directory or file.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Run the workflow, but submit, wait for, cancel and run nothing (see
.BR "DRY RUN" ).
.TP
.B \-\-shell
Start an interactive bash with the directives (see
.BR "INTERACTIVE USE" ).
.TP
.BR \-\-jobs [ =all ]
List pending and running jobs (with
.BR all ,
also finished ones), then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-library
Print the path of the library and exit (first argument only).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print runflow's version; in a Git checkout that is not exactly a release,
also its commit, e.g.
.BR "0.1.0 (git v0.1.0-3-g842f3b2-modified)" .
What changed in each version: CHANGELOG.md.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH DIRECTIVES
.BI "@help @" NAME
shows a directive's options.
There is no
.BI @ NAME " \-\-help\fR:\fP"
.B "@flow \-\-help"
is Flow's own help.
.SS @flow
.B @flow
.RB [ \-v
.IR VERSION ]
.RI [ FLOW_ARGUMENT ]...
.br
.B "@flow \-l"
.RI [ VERSION ]
.br
.B "@flow \-w"
.RI [ VERSION ]
.PP
Runs one Flow process (with MPI:
.BR "@mpi @flow" ),
with its arguments as for
.B flow
itself, and returns its exit status:
.BR "@flow CASE.DATA" ,
.BR "@flow \-\-threads\-per\-process=4 CASE.DATA" ,
.BR "@flow \-\-version" ,
.BR "@flow \-\-help" .
Decks (arguments naming a
.I .DATA
file) are made absolute.
It first reports which Flow runs, e.g.
.BR "[INFO] @flow: flow dev: /opt/opm/dev/bin/flow (OPM 2027.04)" ,
so job logs record it.
Only these options are
.BR @flow 's
own (Flow uses none of them);
.B \-\-
passes even them on:
.TP 4
.BR \-v ", " \-\-flow\-version
The Flow to run, the first that applies: a path; a name in
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS ;
an executable
.BI flow VERSION
or
.I VERSION
in
.B RUNFLOW_FLOW_BIN_PATHS
(by default
.BR PATH );
an OPM version, e.g.
.BR 2025.04 ,
if exactly one build reports it.
Only OPM Flow is used, never another program named flow.
Default:
.BR \-\-flow\-version ,
else
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSION .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List the Flow versions: OPM version, git hash
.RB ( *
marks local changes), build type, build time and Python;
.B *
before a name marks the default.
.B "@flow \-l"
.I VERSION
shows everything about one, including its MPI library and build ID.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-which
Print the executable of a version (default: the default), e.g.
.BR "ldd \(dq$(@flow \-w)\(dq" .
.PP
.BR \-\-flow\-options
are added before the call's arguments.
If Flow embeds Python (for PYACTION),
.B PYTHONPATH
is set to its opm bindings.
How versions, builds and their Python are found: docs/FLOW.md.
.SS @flowpy
.B @flowpy
.RB [ \-v
.IR VERSION ]
.RI [ PYTHON_ARGUMENT ]...
.IR SCRIPT | \fB\-c\fP\ CODE | \fB\-m\fP\ MODULE
.RI [ ARG ]...
.PP
Runs the Python a Flow version's opm bindings
.RB ( opm.io ,
.BR opm.simulators )
were built for, with the bindings in
.BR PYTHONPATH ,
e.g. to prepare decks or read results.
.B \-v
works as for
.BR @flow ;
its default is
.BR \-\-flowpy\-version ,
else
.BR \-\-flow\-version .
Everything else, including
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version ,
goes to Python, after
.BR \-\-flowpy\-options ;
.B \-\-
passes even
.B \-v
on.
It first reports, on standard error, which Python runs, e.g.
.BR "[INFO] @flowpy: flow dev: /usr/bin/python3.13; python 3.13 (from bindings), ..." ,
so job logs record it; standard output stays Python's.
.PP
The Python version comes from
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_PYTHON_MAP ,
else from the bindings found next to Flow, else from the libpython Flow embeds; a different Python is never used.
.SS @mpi
.B @mpi
.RB [ \-n
.IR NP ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR LAUNCHER_OPTIONS ]
.IR PROGRAM | @flow | @flowpy
.RI [ ARG ]...
.PP
Runs a program, or
.BR @flow ,
with
.I NP
MPI ranks (default
.BR "runflow \-n" ;
in a job, the job's
.BR \-n ).
The launcher is
.B RUNFLOW_MPI_LAUNCHER_LOCAL
on this machine and in localq jobs,
.BI RUNFLOW_MPI_LAUNCHER_ SCHEDULER
in jobs of other schedulers; then come
.B \-\-mpi\-options
and
.BR \-o .
.SS @submit
.B @submit
.RB [ \-n
.IR NP ]
.RB [ \-q
.IR QUEUE ]
.RB [ \-\-scheduler
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-depends\-on
.IR ID [, ID ]...]
.RB [ \-\- \fISCHEDULER\fB\-options
.IR OPTS ]...
.RB [ \-o
.IR OUTPUT ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARG ]...
.PP
Queues
.IR COMMAND ,
a program or a directive, as a job with
.I NP
tasks and prints its job ID, e.g.
.BR localq:12 .
The job runs in the directory it was submitted from;
.B \-o
names its output file (default: a per-job log, see
.BR FILES ).
.B \-\-depends\-on
starts it only after these jobs completed, and never if one failed.
.BR \-\-localq\-options ,
.BR \-\-lsf\-options ,
.B \-\-slurm\-options
and
.B \-\-pbs\-options
are used only when the job goes to that scheduler.
Put
.B \-\-
before a command that starts with
.BR \- .
.SS @wait
.B @wait
.RI [ ID ]...
.br
.B @wait
.I @DIRECTIVE
.RI [ ARG ]...
.PP
Waits for the listed jobs, or for all jobs of this run, and returns 1 if any did not complete.
With a directive, e.g.
.BR "@wait @submit @flow CASE.DATA" ,
runs it and waits for the jobs it submits; its output passes through, so
.B job=$(@wait @submit ...)
works.
There is no time limit unless
.B RUNFLOW_WAIT_TIMEOUT
is set; the job keeps running when it expires.
.SS @cancel
.B @cancel
.RI [ ID ]...
.br
.B "@cancel \-\-all"
.PP
Cancels the listed jobs, or with
.B \-\-all
this run's pending and running jobs (in a script, also without arguments).
Returns 1 if a job could not be cancelled, e.g. because it already finished.
.SS @help
.B @help
.RI [ @DIRECTIVE ]
.PP
Lists the loaded directives, or shows one directive's usage, options and settings, with a command that opens this page there.
.SH "OPTION LAYERS"
.B Native options
go to the scheduler: localq takes
.BI \-\-time= LIMIT
.RI ( MINUTES ", " HH : MM : SS ", " DAYS \- HH ...),
.BR \-\-nice [= \fIN\fP],
.BI \-\-job\-name= NAME
and
.BR \-\-exclusive ;
the planned backends will pass theirs to
.BR bsub ,
.B sbatch
or
.BR qsub .
A job gets only those of its own scheduler, so one workflow can carry options for several.
.PP
.B Directive options
.RB ( \-\-flow\-options ,
.BR \-\-mpi\-options ,
\&...) apply to every invocation of a directive, before the call's own.
.PP
Both add up:
.BI RUNFLOW_ NAME _OPTIONS
in the configuration, then
.BI "runflow \-\-" NAME \-options\fR,\fP
then the call's options.
Option strings are split like a shell command line, without expanding anything:
.B \-o \(dq\-\-a=\(aqx y\(aq \-\-b=2\(dq
passes
.B "\-\-a=x y"
and
.BR \-\-b=2 .
.SH INVOCATION
.B "runflow SCRIPT"
runs
.I SCRIPT
in runflow's bash (it need not be executable):
.B $0
is
.BR runflow ,
the script's path is
.BR ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} ,
and variables, loops and
.B job=$(@submit ...)
work as in any bash script.
The exit status is the script's own.
An unknown directive stops it (exit status 127) with a suggestion, e.g.
.BR "Unknown directive: @flwo (did you mean @flow?)" .
On exit and on HUP, INT, PIPE or TERM the run's job list is removed; jobs already submitted keep running.
A script with its own EXIT trap must call
.B runflow::workflow_exit
from it.
.SH SCHEDULERS
With
.BR auto ,
.B runflow
uses localq; the planned backends will be chosen when their commands
.RB ( bsub ,
.BR sinfo ,
.BR qstat )
are in
.BR PATH .
Job IDs name their scheduler
.RB ( localq:12 ),
and a bare ID belongs to the current one; a job can depend only on jobs of its own scheduler.
.SS localq
A queue for one machine and user, without a daemon.
A job uses
.B \-n
of
.B RUNFLOW_LOCALQ_SLOTS
slots (default: the number of CPUs); jobs start first in, first out among those whose dependencies are met, and a job that does not fit waits.
A job whose process dies is FAILED; cancelling kills its process group;
.B @wait
waits without polling.
Its state is kept on local disk (see
.BR FILES ),
so job IDs are local to the machine.
Slots are not enforced: more threads than slots oversubscribe the machine.
.PP
.EX
$ runflow \-\-jobs
localq: slots 3/96 in use
ID     STATUS      NP QUEUE    NAME       DEPENDS EXIT   ELAPSED  COMMAND
13     RUNNING      3 normal   base       \-              0:00:01  @mpi @flow A.DATA
14     PENDING      1 normal   \-          13                   \-  @flowpy compare.py
.EE
.SH ENSEMBLES
Many concurrent runs, e.g. a history matching iteration
.RI ( examples/6_history_match.sh ),
are one job each: the scheduler packs them.
On sites where many small jobs are costly, localq can run them inside one allocation (a pilot job), e.g. with SLURM:
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
#SBATCH \-\-ntasks=64 \-\-time=24:00:00
export RUNFLOW_SCHEDULER=localq RUNFLOW_LOCALQ_SLOTS=$SLURM_NTASKS
export RUNFLOW_LOCALQ_DIR=/var/tmp/runflow\-$(id \-u)/$SLURM_JOB_ID
export RUNFLOW_MPI_LAUNCHER_LOCALQ=\(aqsrun \-\-exact \-\-ntasks={np}\(aq
runflow examples/6_history_match.sh
.EE
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-exact ,
each
.B srun
claims the whole allocation and the runs execute one after another.
.SH "DRY RUN"
.B \-\-dry\-run
runs the workflow but submits, waits for, cancels and runs nothing: it prints each job with a fake ID, checks the directive each job would run (options, deck, Flow version, launcher) and reports problems as warnings, and ends with the number of jobs.
It works for the planned schedulers too.
Plain commands in the script still run; scripts can test
.BR $RUNFLOW_DRY_RUN .
.SH "INTERACTIVE USE"
.B "runflow \-\-shell"
.RI [ OPTION ]...
starts a bash with your
.IR ~/.bashrc ,
the directives and the options applied;
.B exit
leaves it.
In a running bash,
.B "source runflow.bash"
.RI [ OPTION ]...
does the same in place.
.PP
.EX
(runflow localq 2025.04) $ job=$(@submit \-n 8 @mpi @flow CASE.DATA)
(runflow localq 2025.04) $ @flowpy \-c \(aqimport opm.io\(aq
[runflow] Job localq:12 completed
.EE
.PP
The prompt shows the scheduler and the Flow version
.RB ( RUNFLOW_PROMPT=false :
not); Tab completes directives, their options, Flow versions, decks and job IDs; finished jobs are reported before the prompt
.RB ( RUNFLOW_NOTIFY ,
.BR RUNFLOW_NOTIFY_INTERVAL ).
.B runflow
and its man page are added to
.B PATH
and
.B MANPATH
if they are not there yet.
Nothing closes the shell and no traps are set;
.B @cancel
needs job IDs or
.BR \-\-all .
.SH "LIBRARY USE"
A script can load the directives itself, to run also as
.BR ./run.sh :
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
source runflow.bash || exit 1
runflow::setup \-n 8 \-\-flow\-version 2025.04 || exit 1
@mpi @flow "$1"
.EE
.PP
bash finds
.I runflow.bash
through
.BR PATH ,
next to
.BR runflow ;
without
.B PATH
lookup:
.BR "source \(dq$(runflow \-\-library)\(dq" .
.B runflow::setup
takes the options of
.B runflow
(not
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-\-jobs ,
.BR \-h ).
The script then runs as described in
.BR INVOCATION ,
under
.B runflow
too.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings come from, later winning:
.I etc/defaults/runflow.conf
in the runflow directory (every setting, its default and an explanation; not
edited); the files
.I /etc/runflow/runflow.conf
(the machine),
.I etc/runflow.conf
in the runflow directory (the site; not part of runflow's Git repository) and
.I ~/.config/runflow/runflow.conf
(you), or only
.B RUNFLOW_CONFIG
if set; the environment; runflow's options; the directive's options.
Options add to
.BI RUNFLOW_ NAME _OPTIONS\fR;\fP
everything else is replaced.
.PP
The files are bash, read in that order: copy the lines you change from the
defaults file.
A list setting is extended with
.B +=
and replaced with
.BR = .
In
.B RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS
and
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_PYTHON_MAP ,
the last item of a name (or the last matching pattern) wins, so
.B +=
also redefines a name an earlier file set.
A file that someone besides its owner or root can write (everyone, or a
group other than the owner's private one) is refused.
The main settings:
.TP
.B RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS
Named Flow versions, for builds that share an executable name, as
.IB NAME = PATH
items (the executable or its directory), e.g.
.RS
.EX
RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS=(\(aq2025.04=/opt/opm/2025.04/bin/flow\(aq)   # site
RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS+=(\(aqdev=~/opm/build/bin\(aq)                # you
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B RUNFLOW_FLOW_BIN_PATHS
Directories searched for Flow, in order; the entry
.B PATH
(the default) is
.B PATH
when Flow is looked up, in a job after
.BR RUNFLOW_JOB_SETUP .
.TP
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSION ", " RUNFLOW_FLOWPY_VERSION
The version of
.B @flow
and of
.B @flowpy
without
.B \-v
(default
.BR flow ;
empty: the same).
.TP
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_PYTHONPATH ", " RUNFLOW_FLOW_PYTHON_MAP
Where a version's opm bindings are
.RB ( auto ,
a template with
.BR {version} ,
.BR {python} ,
.BR {prefix} ,
or empty), and Python versions per version pattern (e.g.
.BR "(\(aqflow2025*:3.12\(aq)" ).
.TP
.BI RUNFLOW_MPI_LAUNCHER_ SCHEDULER\fR,\fP " RUNFLOW_MPI_LAUNCHER_LOCAL"
MPI launcher, with
.B {np}
for the ranks (default
.BR "mpirun \-np {np}" ;
SLURM:
.BR "srun \-\-ntasks={np}" ).
Binding is the MPI library's default; several runs side by side on one machine need it off, e.g.
.BR "mpirun \-np {np} \-\-bind\-to none" .
.TP
.B RUNFLOW_JOB_SETUP
Shell commands run at the start of every job, e.g. module loads.
.TP
.BI RUNFLOW_DEFAULT_QUEUE_ SCHEDULER\fR,\fP " RUNFLOW_LOCALQ_SLOTS"
Default queues, and localq's slots.
.SS "For administrators"
.IP \(bu 2
Install runflow once, read-only, at a path that is the same on every node, e.g.
.IR /shared/apps/runflow/ VERSION :
jobs find runflow and extension directives at the same path.
.IP \(bu
Put the site's settings in the installation's
.IR etc/runflow.conf :
named Flow versions, search paths,
.B RUNFLOW_JOB_SETUP
(e.g.
.BR "module load opm/2025.04" ),
MPI launchers, queues and accounts.
One file serves every node;
.I /etc/runflow/runflow.conf
is for what differs between machines, and must be kept on each of them.
.IP \(bu
An environment module
.RB ( "module load runflow" )
should only add runflow to
.B PATH
and
.BR MANPATH .
Put the site's defaults in the installation's
.I etc/runflow.conf
instead: a setting the module exports, e.g.
.BR "setenv RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSION 2025.04" ,
comes from the environment, which wins over every user's own configuration
file, so a user's
.B RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSION=dev
in
.I ~/.config/runflow/runflow.conf
would be silently ignored.
In
.IR etc/runflow.conf ,
the same default applies to everyone, and users can still override it.
.IP \(bu
Nothing is enforced: users override or extend every setting in their own
file, e.g. their builds before the site's with
.BR "RUNFLOW_FLOW_BIN_PATHS=(~/opm/bin \(dq${RUNFLOW_FLOW_BIN_PATHS[@]}\(dq)" .
Limits belong to the scheduler.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every setting can also be set in the environment, except the lists
.RB ( RUNFLOW_FLOW_VERSIONS ,
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_BIN_PATHS ,
.BR RUNFLOW_FLOW_PYTHON_MAP :
bash cannot export them), and:
.TP
.B RUNFLOW_CONFIG
The only configuration file to read.
.TP
.B RUNFLOW_DEBUG=true
Print debug messages.
.PP
Workflow scripts share the shell with
.BR runflow ,
so they must not define names starting with
.BR @ ,
.B runflow::
or
.BR RUNFLOW_ .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/runflow/runflow.conf
Your configuration; also
.I /etc/runflow/runflow.conf
and
.I etc/runflow.conf
in the runflow directory.
.TP
.I etc/defaults/runflow.conf
In the runflow directory: every setting, its default and an explanation.
.TP
.I ~/.config/runflow/directives/
Your extension directives.
.TP
.IR ~/.local/state/runflow/logs/localq. HOST . ID .log
The output of a localq job without
.BR \-o .
.TP
.IR /var/tmp/runflow\- UID /localq/
localq's state: a private directory on local disk.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The script's or command's own; 1 for usage and configuration errors; 127 for an unknown directive; 129, 130, 141 or 143 when stopped by HUP, INT, PIPE or TERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
runflow \-n 4 \-c @mpi @flow CASE.DATA            # Flow here, on 4 ranks
runflow \-c @wait @submit \-n 8 @mpi @flow CASE.DATA # a job, waiting for it
runflow \-\-dry\-run \-s slurm workflow.sh            # what it would submit
.EE
.PP
A workflow with two concurrent runs and a comparison that waits for both:
.PP
.EX
a=$(@submit @mpi @flow CASE_A.DATA)
b=$(@submit @mpi @flow CASE_B.DATA)
@submit \-\-depends\-on "$a,$b" @flowpy summary.py FOPR CASE_A CASE_B
@wait
.EE
.PP
More, in order of the concepts they introduce, in
.I examples/
(see its README.md): a local MPI run and its results, two Flow versions, many jobs at once, dependencies and restarts, MPI jobs, and an ES-MDA loop.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
bash 4.4 or newer, coreutils, util-linux and procps; Flow, and an MPI launcher for
.BR @mpi .
On macOS:
.BR "brew install bash flock" ,
with Homebrew's
.I bin
first in
.BR PATH .
Missing tools are reported at startup.
.SH LICENSE
GPL-3.0-or-later: the GNU General Public License, version 3 or (at your
option) any later version; LICENSE in the runflow directory.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mpirun (1),
.BR flock (1).
In the runflow directory: docs/FLOW.md (finding Flow and its Python), docs/DIRECTIVES.md (writing directives), docs/BACKENDS.md (scheduler backends, running on clusters), docs/DEVELOPMENT.md (layout and tests).
